      </ol>
    </nav>

    <h2 id="sec-1">Why the first ten minutes matter most</h2>
    <p>The inspection matters most for the newest riders, and the reasons are plain: a machine you have checked is a machine you know, and knowing it removes the first layer of a first week's nerves, the worry that something you did not look at will surprise you. The inspection converts the machine from a stranger into a known object, and confidence is built exactly that way, one known thing at a time.</p>
    <p>The second reason is money, and it deserves honesty: the rental's deposit is real money held against the machine's condition, and the marks that existed before you, the scuffs, scratches and dents of previous riders, belong to the record rather than to your bill. The ten minutes of photographs at pickup are the protection, and a first rental is precisely when the protection is worth its whole cost, because a new rider's week generates enough new learning without inheriting old damage.</p>
    <p>The third reason is the cluster's caution, and this page keeps it whole: an inspection does not make a new rider an experienced one, and the machine's good condition does not make the traffic simple. The ritual is the foundation of a cautious week, not a substitute for one, and the related pages in this profile carry the rest of that week, the traffic, the timing, the pace, each of them built on the machine this page teaches you to know.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-2">The walk-around, step by step</h2>
    <p>The walk-around is one slow circle, and the steps are in order: start at the front and look at the front tyre, its rubber and the wheel it sits on; move to the handlebars and squeeze the front brake lever, then turn the bars gently from side to side; look at the headlight and the front panels; walk the machine's left side, looking at the panels and footrests; check the stand and its spring; look at the rear tyre and the exhaust; walk the right side back to the front. One circle, two minutes, eyes only.</p>
    <p>The beginner's version of the circle looks for four simple things, and the four are worth memorizing: anything leaking, a dark line under the machine or a wet patch on the engine; anything loose, a wobbling mirror, a rattling panel; anything cracked, the tyre's sidewall, a light's lens, a lever; and anything broken outright. The four categories cover most of what a walk-around finds, and a new rider who knows them is not inspecting blind, they are reading with a small but complete vocabulary.</p>
    <p>The circle ends at the seat, and the seat is lifted if the machine allows: the storage underneath is where the documents and the day's small things live, and looking at it now confirms the space is clean, dry and the helmet's home if the terms say so. The sitting test completes the meeting, feet flat at the ground, hands comfortable at the bars, the machine's weight felt and accepted, and the ritual's first half is done.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-3">Tyres and brakes, the two big checks</h2>
    <p>The tyres are the machine's whole contact with the road, two small patches of rubber that do all the stopping and steering, and the beginner's check is visual and simple: look at each tyre's surface, the tread, the grooves and blocks, should look deep and defined rather than worn smooth at the middle; look at the sidewalls, the sides, for cracks or bulges; press each tyre with a thumb, it should feel firmly rounded rather than soft. A thumb-check takes seconds, and a soft tyre is a finding to mention, not a mystery to ride on.</p>
    <p>The brakes are the second big check, and the rental's two brakes are tested separately: squeeze the right lever firmly while the machine stands still, the lever should feel firm well before it reaches the bars, and the machine should resist being pushed forward while it is held; press the rear brake pedal or lever the same way. Then the moving test, at walking speed in a safe space, each brake alone, then both, feeling the machine stop straight and calm. A first week deserves brakes tested by hand before the traffic tests them for you.</p>
    <p>The two big checks share a rule for new riders, and the rule is the page's firmest sentence: anything that feels wrong at walking speed is worth saying out loud at the counter, because the person handing over the keys has seen a thousand machines and can settle a question in a minute. The <a href="/motorbike-guides/motorbike-maintenance-checklist-hanoi/">maintenance checklist guide</a> covers the mechanical ground in depth for curious readers; the beginner's duty is smaller and clearer, test, notice, ask.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-4">Lights, horn and mirrors</h2>
    <p>The lights are checked with the machine's own switches, and the beginner's version is complete in a minute: turn on the ignition, confirm the headlight shines, then squeeze each brake and check that the brake lights glow, front and rear, the brake lights matter most because they are how the city behind you knows you are stopping. Turn signals, if the machine carries them, blink left and right, and the horn gets a polite test, because in Hanoi the horn is a working word in the traffic's language, not an accessory.</p>
    <p>The mirrors deserve their own paragraph for a new rider, because they are adjustable and the adjustment belongs to you: sit on the machine as you will ride it, and turn each mirror until you can see the road behind and a little of the lane beside you, using the test that a car appearing behind you should appear in the glass without your head moving much. The mirrors the last rider set are aimed for the last rider, and the first five minutes of your week is when yours get set.</p>
    <p>The helmet joins the ritual at this point, because the <a href="/motorbike-guides/motorbike-helmet-guide-hanoi/">helmet guide</a> is right that the head's protection is the inspection's truest subject: the helmet fits snugly without pressure points, the strap fastens properly, the visor is clean, and the helmet is yours for the week, worn every ride, every time. A new rider's inspection that ends with a well-fitted helmet has ended with the most important check of all.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-5">Photographing the marks, the beginner's armor</h2>
    <p>The photographs are the inspection's paperwork and the new rider's armor, and the ritual is simple: before riding away, walk the machine one more time with the phone's camera and photograph every mark you can find, the scuffs on the panels, the scratches near the key, the scrape on the exhaust, the crack in a mirror's housing, each one a small piece of evidence that says this was here before me. The phone's timestamps do the quiet legal work, and the whole album takes five minutes.</p>
    <p>The spoken half of the ritual matters as much as the photographic one, and the new rider does it deliberately: point at each mark and say it out loud to the person at the counter, this scratch, this scuff, so that the machine's condition is acknowledged by both of you before the keys move. A straightforward shop welcomes the ritual, because it protects the shop too, and a first-time renter who does it calmly is treated as a careful renter, which is exactly the reputation a first week deserves.</p>
    <p>The album's afterlife is the point of making it: on the return morning, the photographs are the comparison, each old mark matched to its frame, and the week's honest wear, if any, owned and discussed without mystery. The <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/first-time-visitors-pre-ride-inspection/">first-time visitors' inspection page</a> carries the same ritual for visitors new to the city, and the deposit it protects is the same size for every profile, which is why the ritual is the same.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-6">Asking for a different bike</h2>
    <p>Sometimes the inspection finds something the walk-around cannot accept, and the new rider's version of the finding is simpler than the expert's: a brake that feels soft, a tyre that looks worn smooth, a light that does not work, a leak under the machine. The finding is not a complaint, it is a question, and the question is asked calmly and in plain words, this brake feels soft to me, could we look at it, or could I see another bike.</p>
    <p>The ask is normal, and the fleet's structure makes it easy: the price bands hold several machines each, 150,000 VND for the Wave, Sirius, Click and Mio, and the exchange within a band costs nothing. The shop that takes a finding seriously is the shop a first week deserves, and the shop that waves away a soft brake is telling you something about the whole week, better heard on day one than day five.</p>
    <p>The new rider's version of the ask ends with the re-inspection, and the page is explicit: the new machine gets the same full ritual, the circle, the two big checks, the lights, the photographs, all of it, because the ritual belongs to the machine rather than the calendar. Ten more minutes, and the week's foundation is set on a machine whose every answer you know.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-7">The five-minute morning version</h2>
    <p>The full ritual is for pickup day, and the week's other mornings get the five-minute version, run before the first trip while the coffee cools: a look at both tyres, a squeeze of the front brake, a glance at the ground beneath the machine for overnight leaks, a beep of the horn and a check of the lights, and a slow look around the machine for anything the city added while it slept. The habit costs five minutes and catches the small problems while they are small.</p>
    <p>The morning version has a beginner's specific gift, and it is the one this page most wants to leave: the habit builds the eye. A rider who has looked at their machine every morning for a week knows it deeply, notices changes instantly, and carries the beginning of the judgment this cluster's other pages teach. The inspection is not a chore on the way to riding, it is riding's first skill, learned in five-minute installments.</p>
    <p>The closing picture is the first day's whole arc: a new rider at the counter, the ritual run slowly and honestly, the marks photographed and spoken, the helmet fitted, the five-minute habit armed for the mornings after, and the quiet ride away on a machine whose every answer is known. The week that follows is built on that foundation, and the rider who built it deliberately has done the most experienced thing a beginner can do, which is to know their machine before the city asks them to.</p>

    <h2>Frequently asked questions about pre-ride inspections for new riders</h2>
    <article class="faq-entry">
      <h2>How does a new rider inspect a rental motorbike before riding it in Hanoi?</h2>
      <p>With the ten-minute ritual: one slow walk-around circle looking for leaks, looseness, cracks and breaks; a thumb-check of both tyres and a firm squeeze of each brake, then a walking-speed stop test; the lights, horn and mirror checks; the helmet fitted; and photographs of every existing mark, spoken aloud at the counter. The ritual assumes no prior knowledge, and the five-minute morning version keeps the eye sharp all week.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="faq-entry">
      <h2>Why should a new rider photograph a rental motorbike before riding away?</h2>
      <p>Because the photographs are the deposit's armor: every mark that existed before the ride, the scuffs, scratches and dents of previous riders, belongs to the record rather than the bill, and the timestamps turn the album into evidence. Pointing at each mark and saying it aloud at the counter makes the record shared, protects an honest shop as much as it protects you, and turns the return morning into a comparison rather than a memory contest.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="faq-entry">
      <h2>What should a new rider do if the rental motorbike fails inspection?</h2>
      <p>Ask calmly and in plain words for it to be looked at, or for another machine in the same price band, because the bands hold several machines each and the exchange costs nothing. A soft brake, a worn tyre, a dead light or a leak are findings, not complaints, and the shop's response tells you about the whole week ahead. The replacement machine gets the full ritual again, photographs and all.</p>
    </article>

